{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And if the ox was a gorer.</b> In Bava Kamma, chapter כיצד הרגל, Abaye and Rava disagree. Abaye says: 'yesterday one, the day before yesterday two, the third warning, and he did not guard it — it comes to a fourth goring.' Rava says: 'yesterday, the day before yesterday one, the third warning, and he did not guard it — here is a goring; he is liable.' And it must be that Rava means when it gores a fourth goring, as we say in the chapter חזקת הבתים: say that an ox known to gore is not liable until a fourth goring — this implies that everyone agrees to this. Rather, Abaye and Rava disagree on this point: Abaye derives all four gorings from the verse itself, while Rava derives only three from the verse. And if you ask: what difference does it make? Rabbi Ezra the prophet, of blessed memory, says: the practical difference of Abaye, who derives four gorings from the verse itself, is that according to Rabbi Yehudah he is not liable unless the fourth goring was on a single day by itself, after three gorings; but according to Rava, even if the fourth goring was on the same day as the third goring, he is liable."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:52.093583021Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}